---
import Document from '../layouts/Document.astro';
import { site, safeExternalUrl, workflow, troubleshooting, localUrl } from '../data/site';
const sections = [{id:'installation',title:'Installation'},{id:'studio-install',title:'Studio file installation'},{id:'first-conversion',title:'Your first conversion'},{id:'image-mapping',title:'Images & mappings'},{id:'sizing',title:'Choose a sizing mode'},{id:'interactions',title:'Interactions & masks'},{id:'limits',title:'Compatibility notes'},{id:'troubleshooting',title:'Troubleshooting'}];
---
<Document title="From frame to forge" description="Install the plugins, export your root frame, map image assets, and make the final interface your own in Roblox Studio." sections={sections}>
  <section id="installation"><h2>Get FrameForge</h2><p>Install FrameForge in Figma and Roblox Studio. Use verified official listings when available, or download the Studio plugin file below.</p><div class="install-panels"><div class="install-panel"><h3>For Figma</h3><p>Add FrameForge from its official Community listing and run it in your design file.</p><p class="availability">OFFICIAL LISTING LINK PENDING</p><a href={safeExternalUrl(site.links.figmaCommunity)} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Browse Figma Community</a></div><div class="install-panel"><h3>For Roblox Studio</h3><p>The complete {site.downloads.studio.version} alpha plugin is available as a file. No build tools are required.</p><a class="button secondary" href={localUrl(site.downloads.studio.path)} download={site.downloads.studio.fileName}>Download Studio plugin</a><p><a href="#studio-install">How to install the file</a></p></div></div><p>If you need help finding the official Figma listing, contact <strong>@mngr06 on Discord</strong>. Source builds and maintainer setup are documented separately in <code>docs/install-and-share.md</code>.</p></section>
<section id="studio-install"><h2>Install the Studio plugin file</h2><ol><li>Download <strong>{site.downloads.studio.fileName}</strong> from this site.</li><li>In Studio, open <strong>Plugins → Plugins Folder</strong>. Keep the folder open, then close Studio.</li><li>Move the downloaded file into that folder. Keep the complete model intact.</li><li>Reopen Studio and select <strong>Plugins → FrameForge</strong>. The importer header should show version <strong>{site.downloads.studio.version}</strong>.</li></ol><p>Already installed another FrameForge copy? Disable it in <strong>Manage Plugins</strong> so only one copy runs.</p><details><summary>Cannot find Plugins Folder?</summary><p>On Windows, the usual local folder is <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Roblox\Plugins</code>. Prefer the folder Studio opens, because the location can be configured. You can also insert the complete model into a temporary place and use <strong>Save as Local Plugin</strong> on its plugin container, preserving the included modules.</p></details><details><summary>Verify the downloaded file</summary><p>The website provides the same model as the project’s {site.downloads.studio.version} release package. Its SHA-256 checksum is:</p><p><code>{site.downloads.studio.sha256}</code></p><p><a href={localUrl(site.downloads.studio.path + '.sha256')} download>Download the checksum file</a>.</p></details><p>When the first import installs generated interactions or the hybrid fitting script, Studio may ask permission to manage scripts. Allow FrameForge and retry if the import report says it rolled back. Turn off runtime installation when you want UI without generated interactions.</p></section>
  <section id="first-conversion"><h2>Your first conversion</h2>{workflow.map((step,index) => <div class="guide-step"><span class="workflow-number">{String(index+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><div><h3>{step.title}</h3><p>{step.text}</p></div></div>)}<p>Save behavior and element settings before changing selection. Select the complete root again before exporting. In Studio, use <strong>Validate export</strong>, then <strong>Forge into StarterGui</strong>. Each import creates a new GUI, so review duplicates before keeping a new version.</p><figure class="guide-diagram" aria-label="Conversion path"><b>Root frame</b><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><b>Export JSON + images</b><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><b>Native Studio UI</b><figcaption>Figma creates the export. The Studio plugin creates the objects.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section id="image-mapping"><h2>Give images a Roblox asset ID</h2><p>Native objects do not need image uploads. Image fills, unsupported graphics, and text fallbacks may appear in the export’s collected assets.</p><ol><li>Open <strong>Assets</strong> in the Figma plugin and download the current PNG for each unmapped image.</li><li>Upload it through Roblox’s own asset tools using an account with the right permissions.</li><li>Wait for processing and check the asset is available to your intended experience.</li><li>Enter the numeric Roblox asset ID for the matching image key, then copy the updated JSON.</li><li>Review Studio’s import report for any missing mappings.</li></ol><p>Keep archival image bytes off for a smaller Studio paste transfer unless you specifically need them. A mapping identifies the Roblox image; it is not a copy of the image inside Studio.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Changed artwork can need a fresh upload</strong><p>Rendered text, cropped or filtered fills, and rasterized masks use image-content fingerprints. After editing that artwork, upload the new PNG and map its new key. Do not reuse an older bitmap just because the layer name is the same.</p></div><p>Automatic upload through a connected personal account is prepared but not live. <a href={localUrl('account/')}>Read the account-linking status.</a></p></section>
  <section id="sizing"><h2>Choose a sizing mode</h2><table class="compatibility-table"><caption>Pick sizing for the interface you are building.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Mode</th><th scope="col">Behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Pixel</th><td>Keeps authored pixel dimensions. Useful when fixed geometry is intentional.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Responsive</th><td>Uses compatible constraints and scale-based sizing. Check the resulting layout across target viewports.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Hybrid</th><td>Fits the authored root uniformly while preserving aspect ratio and spacing. It can shrink to fit and does not enlarge beyond the authored size.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Static hybrid imports use a small fitting LocalScript. Behavior-enabled imports use one bundled runtime. Static pixel and responsive imports need no scripts. If runtime installation is disabled, you still get editable UI, but generated interactions and fitting behavior will not run.</p></section>
  <section id="interactions"><h2>Configure motion deliberately</h2><p>Use the interactions and effects controls on your selected layer. Preview a preset, choose its trigger, tune its distance, timing, and easing, and save. A selected-design preview is a read-only image of the selected layer; complete game behavior still needs Play-mode testing.</p><p><strong>Mouse hover</strong> reacts to entering and leaving the hover source. <strong>Loop</strong> repeats explicitly. Hover Tilt is 2D rotation, not a perspective transform. Reexport after changing a trigger; an older export can preserve its previous behavior.</p><h3>Keep the mask still</h3><p>For decorative stripes, animate the inner frame and keep the clipping container stationary. Moving the clipping box with its content cannot clip the content against the box’s old position.</p><figure class="guide-diagram"><b>Button / hover source</b><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><b>Stationary mask</b><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><b>Moving stripe frame</b><figcaption>In Figma’s Layers panel, put the mask below the layers it clips.</figcaption></figure><p>Use the parent as the hover source where appropriate, or reference a unique layer identifier inside the exported root. An empty-mask warning may offer <strong>Fix mask order</strong> for one unambiguous frame-and-mask pair. Review the change and use Figma Undo if necessary. Ambiguous groups need manual repair.</p><p><a href={localUrl('#playground')}>Try the browser interaction example.</a></p></section>
  <section id="limits"><h2>Know the boundaries</h2><table class="compatibility-table"><caption>Supported behavior depends on the source layer and configuration.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Design detail</th><th scope="col">Conversion approach</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Frames & layouts</th><td>Native containers, compatible auto layout, grids, padding, constraints, and scrolling.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Text</th><td>Supported font families and faces stay editable. Unavailable families or styles become individual text images with warnings.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Masks</th><td>Compatible geometric clipping keeps editable children. Complex masks rasterize their affected scope.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Graphics</th><td>Supported shapes, corners, strokes, and linear gradients become native UI. Complex vectors need image assets.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Shadows & blur</th><td>Supported static filled shapes can use bounded drop-shadow approximations. Inner shadows, layer blur, and interactive shadows are outside this milestone.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Runtime behavior</th><td>Configured actions, text bindings, hover/press effects, entrances, ripples, and supported idle effects share a bundled client runtime.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Text rendered as an image cannot receive input or update through live text bindings. Use supported native text for those cases. Live synchronization, component factories, full perspective transforms, and custom ProximityPrompt UI are outside this milestone.</p><p>Roblox processing, moderation, ownership, and experience permissions can affect image availability. Always test visual layout, input, interactions, and cleanup in Studio before releasing your experience.</p></section>
  <section id="troubleshooting"><h2>When something needs a second pass</h2>{troubleshooting.map(item => <details><summary>{item.title}</summary><p>{item.text}</p></details>)}<p>Still stuck? <a href={localUrl('support/')}>Send the creator a useful reproduction.</a></p></section>
</Document>
